From 19387f5f24644f456e1f0247181f743ebe7a3b05 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 15:12:19 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] feat(agent_ui): pin a pending question above the composer,
 one at a time

A question the agent asks arrives as a session-scoped form elicitation, and
Zed renders it as an entry in the transcript. It therefore scrolls away with
the conversation: the turn is blocked waiting for an answer the user can no
longer see, and the only route back is scrolling to find it. The VS Code
extension does not do this -- it puts the pending request in a container
pinned in the chat view, one request at a time.

Zed already has that surface and already fills it with this exact card.
`ThreadView::render` calls `render_request_elicitations` immediately before
`render_message_editor`, and `ElicitationCard` draws both. What never reached
it is the session-scoped half: `handle_create_elicitation` routes
`ElicitationScope::Session` into the thread's entry list and `Connection`
into the store the pinned renderer reads. So this is a second reader of the
first list, not a second renderer.

  * `agent.elicitation_placement` chooses between the two, and the panel's
    options menu carries both values under an "Agent Questions" header. Two
    entries of one setting rather than one checkbox: the choice is between
    two presentations, and a checkbox would have to name one of them and
    leave the reader to infer the other. It defaults to `card`, which is the
    one behaviour change here -- `inline` is upstream's, unaltered, and one
    click away.

  * Only a *pending form* moves. A URL elicitation is a link to follow rather
    than a question to answer, and a resolved one is already history; pinning
    either would park something above the composer that nothing is waiting on.
    Both renderers read one predicate, `renders_elicitation_as_card`, so
    neither can draw a request the other has drawn too.

  * The cards go in an identified container. The element ids inside a card are
    keyed by its index, and the connection-scoped cards rendered as their
    neighbours are keyed by an index into a different list -- an id path of
    their own is what stops index 0 of one colliding with index 0 of the other.

  * `AcpThread::elicitations()` is new, mirroring what `ElicitationStore`
    already exposes for the connection-scoped list. The alternative is walking
    `entries()` -- the whole transcript -- on every frame the panel draws, to
    find the handful of entries that are elicitations at all.

Pagination is the second half, and it is deliberately narrow.

The adapter packs every question of one AskUserQuestion call into a single
form: `question_<n>` for the question and `question_<n>_custom` for its own
"Other" box, the latter marked with an `_askUserQuestionCustomAnswer` `_meta`
naming the field it belongs to. That marker is un-namespaced on purpose, so
any client can pair the two, and reading it here is what lets a page hold a
question together with its own free-text field.

A form that marks *no* companion is not split at all. Nothing else says "these
fields are separate questions", and an MCP server asking for a host and a port
is a form to read together -- a wizard over it would hide each field from the
other for nothing. So an ordinary form comes back as one page and a paginated
card renders exactly like an unpaginated one.

Two smaller decisions inside it:

  * The pages live on `ElicitationFormState`, computed once from the schema it
    is already handed. Recomputing them at render would let the page index and
    the grouping disagree; owning them means `set_page` can clamp and nothing
    can point past the end.

  * `set_errors` moves to the first page holding an error. Submit is only
    reachable from the last page, so an error on an earlier one would
    otherwise reject the form while showing nothing that explains it.

Submission is unchanged: the accept button still sends one response with every
field, so this is presentation only -- no protocol change, and nothing the
adapter has to know about.

The two `AgentSettings` struct literals in test modules (`agent_ui`,
`agent::tool_permissions`) gain the new field; they construct the struct
exhaustively rather than from defaults.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
